.TH HLSE 1 "2026-06-08" "HLSE 0.9.15" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hlse \- Human-Layer Security Engine: phishing, scam, and threat detection
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hlse_core
.I url
.br
.B hlse_core
.B text
.I "message"
.br
.B hlse_core
.B scan
.I directory
.RB [ --json | --sarif ]
.br
.B hlse_core
.B protect
.I path
.RB [ --ransomware | --smb | --mbr | --net ]
.br
.B hlse_core
.RB [ --json | --quiet | --stdin | --self-test | --benchmark | --version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hlse
is a zero-dependency security detection engine written in pure C. It
detects phishing URLs, scam and social-engineering text, leaked
credentials, ransomware activity, supply-chain typosquats, file
masquerade, and system-hardening gaps. All analysis is performed locally:
the tool makes no network calls and collects no telemetry.
.PP
Each scan produces a score from 0 to 100 mapped to an action:
.RS
.TP
.B 0\(en14
SAFE \(em no action
.TP
.B 15\(en39
LOG \(em record for review
.TP
.B 40\(en59
ALERT \(em warn the user
.TP
.B 60\(en79
BLOCK \(em prevent the action
.TP
.B 80\(en100
ISOLATE \(em quarantine immediately
.RE
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.I url
Scan a URL for phishing: homoglyphs, typosquatting, suspicious TLDs,
subdomain spoofing, IP-host brand abuse, percent-encoded paths,
brand-hyphen patterns, and high-entropy (DGA) domains.
.TP
.B text \fI"message"\fR
Scan text for scam patterns: urgency, financial bait, authority
impersonation, ransom and BEC language, across English, Japanese,
Chinese, and Korean. Resists zero-width, homoglyph, HTML-entity,
l33tspeak, and full-width evasion.
.TP
.B scan \fIdirectory\fR
Recursively scan a directory for leaked secrets, file masquerade, and
embedded phishing URLs. Skips vendor and build directories. Intended for
CI/CD use.
.TP
.B scan \fIdirectory\fR " --git-history"
Scan every commit ever made to the repository at
.I directory
for leaked secrets \[em] not just the current working tree. A credential
that was committed and later deleted is still readable by anyone who clones
the repository; a working-tree-only scan never sees it, but this does.
Streams
.B git log --all -p
(one subprocess for the whole history, spawned via
.BR fork (2)/\fBexecve\fR(2)
with the directory passed as a discrete argument \[em] never through a
shell, so no path can inject a command) and scans only added lines. Requires
.I directory
to be a git repository; a non-repository path is a usage error (exit 2).
.B git log
performs no network I/O, so this does not affect HLSE's zero-network-calls
guarantee.
.TP
.B protect \fIpath\fR
Behavioral protection checks: ransomware entropy and ransom-note
detection, SMB canary files, mounted network drives, and MBR/GPT
integrity (the last needs root and a device path).
.TP
.B esp \fR[\fIpath\fR]
Scan the EFI System Partition (default
.IR /boot/efi )
for .efi binaries containing ransom/bootkit strings \(em UEFI-era boot
integrity, complementing the legacy MBR check. Read-only; never follows
symlinks.
.TP
.B secret \fI"text"\fR\fR | \fB--stdin\fR
Scan a text blob or standard input for leaked credentials (AWS, GitHub,
Stripe, Slack, SSH keys, .env passwords), excluding documented
placeholder/example keys.
.TP
.B email \fI"headers"\fR\fR | \fB--stdin\fR
Email-header forensics: SPF/DKIM failure, Reply-To mismatch, display-name
spoofing, and free-mail corporate impersonation (BEC).
.TP
.B clipboard \fI"copied"\fR \fI"pasted"\fR
Compare a copied and a pasted string; if both are crypto addresses of the
same type but differ, flag a clipboard hijack, escalating when the
replacement was ground to share the original's leading/trailing characters.
.TP
.B package \fIname\fR [\fIecosystem\fR]
Check a package name for typosquatting against known popular packages
(pip, npm, cargo, go, gem).
.TP
.B package --manifest \fIfile\fR [\fIecosystem\fR]
Check every dependency declared in a manifest
.RB ( requirements.txt ", " package.json ,
etc.). The ecosystem is inferred from the filename or given explicitly.
Emits one verdict per suspicious package and a
.B manifest_summary
line; exits 1 if any package reaches the
.B --fail-on
threshold.
.TP
.B paste \fI"command"\fR
Detect pastejacking and dangerous shell one-liners (e.g. curl piped to a
shell).
.TP
.B network
Check ARP, DNS, and hosts-file state for tampering.
.TP
.B file \fIpath\fR
Detect file masquerade: double extensions, magic-byte mismatch, polyglot
payloads, and bidi-override filenames. Analyzes the name even if the file
is absent.
.TP
.B audit
Audit system hardening: SSH configuration, file permissions, DNS, and
cron.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --json
Emit machine-readable JSON instead of human-readable output.
.TP
.B --sarif
Emit SARIF 2.1.0 (GitHub code-scanning compatible). Supported by the
.B scan
command.
.TP
.BR -q ", " --quiet
Suppress output; communicate only through the exit code (CI/CD mode).
.TP
.B --stdin
Pipe mode: read one input per line from standard input.
.TP
.BI --baseline " file"
For the
.B scan
command: suppress every finding whose fingerprint is listed in
.IR file ,
so a brownfield repository's already-accepted findings do not fail the CI
gate. Only findings absent from the baseline count toward the exit code. An
unreadable
.I file
is a usage error (exit 2). Generate a baseline with
.BR --fingerprints .
.TP
.B --fingerprints
For the
.B scan
command: emit one stable fingerprint per finding (16 hex chars, plus its
pattern id and relative path) instead of the normal verdict, and exit 0.
Redirect to a file to create a baseline:
.B hlse_core --fingerprints scan . > .hlse-baseline\fR.
The fingerprint omits the line number, so a finding that moves lines stays
suppressed. An inline
.B hlse:allow
token on a scanned line also suppresses findings on that line.
.TP
.BI --patterns " file"
Load organization-specific secret patterns from
.I file
without a rebuild. Each non-comment, non-blank line is:
.RS
.IP
.B SECRET \fIprefix min_suffix charset score label...\fR
.RE
.IP
.I charset
is one of
.BR alnum ", " alnum_dash ", " hex ", " alpha ", " digit .
.I label
is free text to end of line. A malformed line is skipped with a warning; an
unreadable
.I file
is a usage error (exit 2). Custom patterns are checked with the same
matching and placeholder-suppression logic as the built-in table, and never
affect the corpus benchmark
.RB ( --benchmark ).
Example:
.RS
.IP
.B SECRET ACME_KEY_ 20 alnum 85 ACME Internal API Key
.RE
.TP
.B --self-test
Run the built-in test suite.
.TP
.B --benchmark
Run the in-distribution corpus benchmark and print F1.
.TP
.BR -V ", " --version
Print version and exit.
.TP
.BR -h ", " --help
Show usage and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
No threat (score below 60).
.TP
.B 1
Threat detected (score 60 or above).
.TP
.B 2
Usage error (bad arguments or inaccessible path).
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Scan a suspicious URL:
.B hlse_core https://paypa1.com/signin
.TP
Scan text from the clipboard:
.B xclip -o | hlse_core --stdin
.TP
Pre-commit secret scan emitting SARIF:
.B hlse_core --sarif scan .
.SH PRIVACY
HLSE performs all detection locally. It makes no network connections,
writes no telemetry, and reads no files outside the paths given on the
command line. This is enforced in CI by a tripwire that fails the build
if any network syscall appears in the source.
